
  <sn><md>#### What is soil catena?

**Ans:**

- There can be `huge variations` in soil types ++across a small area++. This can be seen through a `section diagram` ++across an altitude range++ – called a **‘catena’**. 

- Catena shows `how soils can change down slope` although there are `no changes in climatic conditions or parent material`.

- In a catena, the `sequence of soil profiles` which develop down the slope relate to the `relief and altitude` of the land. 

- ++Changing slope++ alters `drainage`, and the `leaching` and `gleying processes`, 
  whilst ++altitude++ strongly influences the `climatic effects` in soil formation.

![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-emon/QDoc/main/SES3/Pedology/LAB/resources/Soil-Catena-Diagram.png)


- A **soil catena** is a `sequence of different soil profiles` that occur down a `slope`. 

- Soil catena may be defined as a `group of soils` occurring in association developed from `same parent material` under `same climatic conditions` but `differ in` their characteristics due to `relief and drainage` conditions.

- **Example:** Sara, Gopalpur, Ishurdi, Gheior are common catena in Ganges floodplain area in Bangladesh.
